markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Filters, Labels, and What Gets Seen

#### When you browse a website or app, filters help narrow down large lists of items.

### But filters don’t just organize information. They also shape what feels easy to find and what gets overlooked.

## Two Websites, Same Goal
#### Below are two websites that sell similar products.
#### Both allow users to filter results, but they do it differently.

<div style="background:#f7f8fa; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; margin:18px auto; max-width:85%;">

  <p style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:0;">
    💡 <strong>Look at each retail website’s filtering system.</strong>
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li style="font-size:14pt;">Which do you prefer?</li>
    <li style="font-size:14pt;">Which would be easier to use?</li>
  </ul>

</div>

 

<img src="https://images.code.org/8450f6ede3493d945da1a84d3313bea4-image-1770049168398.png" alt="Two shopping websites with different filter options" width="650px" style="display:block; margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto; border-radius:10px;">

<details style="background-color:#ececec; padding:12px; margin:18px auto; border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:8px; max-width:85%;">
  <summary style="font-size:1.4em; font-weight:bold;">🔍 Check your thinking</summary>

<div style="margin-top:12px; line-height:140%;">

<p style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:0;">
      Take a closer look at the filters each site offers:
    </p>

<ul>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;">
        <strong>Website 1</strong> has many filters (Deals, Rating, Occasion, Price, Category)
      </li>
      <li style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:8px;">
        <strong>Website 2</strong> has fewer filters and simpler labels
      </li>
    </ul>

<p style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:12px;">
      <strong>This design choice affects:</strong>
    </p>

<ul>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;">What items appear first</li>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;">What feels easy vs. hard to find</li>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;">Which users feel supported — and which feel overwhelmed</li>
    </ul>

  </div>
</details>


<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5; border-radius:10px; padding:14px; margin:18px auto; max-width:85%;"> <p style="font-size:14pt; margin:0; line-height:135%;"><strong>Design tradeoff:</strong> More filters can help some users search precisely, but can make others feel lost or overloaded.</p> </div>

---


## Example

<div style="background:#f7f8fa; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; margin:18px auto; max-width:85%; display:flex; gap:18px; align-items:flex-start;">



  <!-- Text content -->
 <div>
 <p style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:0;">
      A user searching for <strong>Deals</strong> or <strong>Occasion</strong> may succeed on Website 1.
    </p>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
      That same user may struggle on Website 2, not because the items don’t exist, but because the filters don’t.
    </p>

 <p style="font-size:14pt; margin-bottom:0;">
      <strong>👉</strong> Filters don’t just show things, they also exclude things.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>



## Key Terms

<div style="display:flex; gap:14px; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; margin-top:16px;"> <div style="background:#e4d7f0; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:360px; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);"> <strong style="font-size:14pt;">Threshold</strong> <p style="font-size:14pt;"> A <strong>threshold</strong> is a cutoff value used to decide which items pass a filter. </p> <p style="font-size:14pt;"> <strong>Example:</strong> An art site only features artists with at least <strong>500 followers</strong>. The threshold is 500. </p> </div>

<div style="background:#f7bfbf; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:360px; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);"> <strong style="font-size:14pt;">Fallback value</strong> <p style="font-size:14pt;"> A <strong>fallback value</strong> is a default label or value used when data is missing or doesn’t match a category. </p> <p style="font-size:14pt;"> <strong>Example:</strong> If a user doesn’t enter an email, the app shows <em>Email: Not provided</em> instead of leaving it blank. </p> </div> </div>



## Next up
#### You’ll explore how filters and thresholds work in code — and how small design choices can have big impacts on who gets seen.

MARKDOWN
